bridge/codex/ — talking to `codex app-server`
=============================================

Tested Codex versions: >=0.172.0 <0.173.0

That range is also TESTED in protocol.js, and test/codex-rpc.test.js fails when
the two disagree. A Codex outside it may work; it has not been looked at. The
plan (docs/plans/23-codex.md) has the bridge show a banner in that case rather
than refuse.

What is here. The first three are Phase 0 of the plan; items.js is Phase 2's,
shared by the rollout reader and the process adapter; models.js is Phase 3's; quota.js and
version.js are Phase 4's.
A conversation's own app-server is spawned by the runner from the adapter
(bridge/runtimes/codex/process.js), which reuses protocol.js and items.js and
frames nothing itself; client.js serves an app-server outside any conversation
(models.js, the recorder, the replay test):

  rpc.js       JSON-RPC 2.0 over a readable and a writable. Newline-delimited
               JSON, an id → promise map, a handler table for server requests,
               and log-once-and-drop for notifications this build does not know.
               Knows nothing about processes, so it works over the session
               host's relayed socket as well as over a child's stdio.
  client.js    Spawns `codex app-server --listen stdio://` (CODEX_BIN, which is
               TGXCODE_CODEX_BIN or `codex`) and does the initialize /
               initialized handshake with experimentalApi on.
  protocol.js  The tested range, and the ServerNotification / ServerRequest
               method lists copied from the schema for that version.
  models.js    `model/list` for the pickers (GET /api/models?runtime=codex),
               asked of a short-lived app-server and cached ten minutes.
  quota.js     The Codex meter and whether Codex is signed in
               (`runtimes.codex` on GET /api/quota): `account/read` and
               `account/rateLimits/read` from a short-lived app-server, with
               `account/rateLimits/updated` from live conversations over them.
  version.js   `codex --version` against npm's @openai/codex and against the
               tested range above (GET /api/codex-version).
  plan.js      `update_plan` as TodoWrite's list. 0.172.0 writes the checklist
               to disk only inside the code-mode `exec` script that called it, so
               this reads JavaScript literals (and names bound to them earlier in
               the script); `turn/plan/updated` is the wire's copy. Phase 4.
  skills.js    `skills/list {cwds}` for the slash palette, cached per directory,
               and `lookup` for the adapter, which sends a chosen skill as a
               `{type:"skill"}` input. Phase 4.
  items.js     The v2 ThreadItem kinds as the bridge's `tool` events, in one
               table. `fromRollout` turns the rollout's form of an item
               (`item_completed`: PascalCase type, snake_case fields, a
               `file://` cwd, argv arrays, a changes map) into the wire's
               (`item/completed`), and everything after it reads the wire's,
               so bridge/runtimes/codex/transcript.js and the Phase 3 adapter
               share it. test/codex-transcript.test.js checks that each
               recorded item reads the same from either side.

Only Node built-ins, like everything else under bridge/.

Upgrading Codex
---------------

1. `npm i -g @openai/codex@<new>` and `codex --version`.
2. Regenerate the schema beside the old one, both variants:

     codex app-server generate-json-schema --experimental --out /tmp/s-exp
     codex app-server generate-json-schema --out /tmp/s-stable
     mkdir -p docs/codex-schema/<new>/{experimental,stable}
     cp /tmp/s-exp/codex_app_server_protocol.schemas.json    docs/codex-schema/<new>/experimental/
     cp /tmp/s-stable/codex_app_server_protocol.schemas.json docs/codex-schema/<new>/stable/

   Only the combined bundle is committed. The generator also writes one file
   per type (about 6 MB more), all of which is in the bundle.
3. `diff` the two versions' bundles. Renamed or removed methods are the ones
   that break us; new notifications are harmless until we want them.
4. Update TESTED and the two lists in protocol.js, and the range above.
5. Re-record the fixtures (test/fixtures/codex/record.js --list), read them
   before committing (the repo is public; the recorder scrubs what it knows
   about), and run `node test/codex-rpc.test.js` and
   `node test/codex-quota.test.js` (which reads the recorded token counts).
6. Re-check the plan's *Measured on* table, and move what changed into it.

Fit with the runtime contract
-----------------------------

Phase 1 put the process contract in the header of
bridge/runtimes/claude/process.js. Checked against it on 0.172.0, the
Codex adapter (Phase 3) can be written inside it except where noted.

Fits as it is:

  - Framing. The runner splits lines and parses them; Codex is
    newline-delimited JSON both ways. So the adapter takes parsed objects,
    and rpc.js's stream handling is not used for a conversation.
  - Correlated requests. The runner mints string ids (`req_<tag>_<n>`), and
    Codex's RequestId is `string | integer`. `request(id, verb, payload)` is
    `{id, method: verb, params: payload}`, and a message with `id` and no
    `method` is a `response` event.
  - Unknown messages give an empty event list. Use protocol.js's
    KNOWN_NOTIFICATIONS for "unknown" and log once, as rpc.js does.
  - Server requests are asks: requestApproval becomes `ask`, and
    `serverRequest/resolved` becomes `ask-withdrawn`, but only for an ask still
    unanswered, because Codex sends it after every answer. Anything not
    handled becomes `request`, and `refuse()` writes a -32601.
  - Lifecycle. `turn/start` and `turn/steer` take `clientUserMessageId`, and
    the userMessage item has a `clientId` field, so `lifecycle` should be able
    to name the runner's `uuid`. This is unmeasured: the fixtures send no
    clientUserMessageId, and `clientId` is null in all of them.
  - setPermissionMode needs no request; it settles at once and the adapter
    sends the new settings on the next turn/start (D5). withdraw() is always
    false, since Codex cannot take back a steered message.

Needed a contract change, and now has one (Phase 3's first step; each is a
case in test/runtime-contract.test.js, against a fake runtime that behaves like
app-server in that respect):

  1. The handshake gates the queue. An adapter that says `handshakeGates` is
     handed the start options (`handshake(o)`, the same `o` as argv), and the
     runner stays `starting` and writes no turn, queued or handed over, until
     the promise resolves. A rejection is fatal: the process is ended and the
     queue handed back as a `send-failed` with the rejection's `kind` and
     `message`. Claude's adapter says false, and nothing about it changed.
  2. A runtime can mint the session's id. `init` carries `minted: true` for
     the id of the session this start created; the runner and pool re-key as
     for a fork, but emit `minted` rather than `forked`. What the runner was
     created with is a placeholder. The create route waits for `minted` and
     answers with the minted id, labelling and noting that one (Phase 3).
  3. `init` may be built from replies. Events still come only from read(),
     which is the point: an `init` said from read() of the thread/start reply
     is rebuilt by the adoption replay, and one pushed from a promise would
     not be. The adapter learns which reply is which from request(id, verb),
     or from the reply's shape on a replay. `raw` may be null.
  4. Turns are requests. turn(entry, {handOver}) may return a request with an
     id from the adapter's own space (`turn/start` when handOver is false,
     `turn/steer` when true), and the runner drops a `response` for any id it
     did not mint — always true, now written down and tested. Never mint an id
     that could be `req_<tag>_<n>`.
  5. A refused steer is re-sent. A new lifecycle state, `returned`: the entry
     goes back to unhanded at the front of the queue and is flushed as a turn of
     its own once nothing is running. The adopt replay knows it too.
  6. An ask may carry `decisions`, {id, label, behavior, scope:
     once|session|always, endsTurn, raw}. A client answers with `choice: id`
     (POST /permission), and the old allow / allow-always / deny body still
     works, mapped onto the list or refused 400 where the list has no
     equivalent. Every answer the bridge gives by itself (stop, auto-deny,
     supersede) picks the nearest entry, so answer() always gets a `choice` on
     such an ask; `raw` never leaves the bridge. The amendment is
     `scope: "always"`, and is not remembered as a session allow of ours.
     web/'s tool card draws one button per entry (Phase 3).
  7. A question's answers reach answer() as `[{id, question, answers:
     [string]}]`, and the adapter says them in its runtime's shape. A client
     may key `answers` by a question's `id` as well as its text, and give
     several strings. Claude's adapter builds the same updatedInput the runner
     used to.

The adapter (bridge/runtimes/codex/process.js, Phase 3) does the rest: it keeps
the integer server-request id as `ask.rpcId` and answers with it, and withdraws a
card on `serverRequest/resolved` only while it is unanswered. Phase 3 added two
optional hooks of its own — `notify` (a line nobody waits on: `initialized`) and
`adopting(o)` — and asks with `followUp: true`, which the runner answers by sending
the next turn; the contract's header in bridge/runtimes/claude/process.js says how.
